  "brief_md": "## O05 — Friend-sourced prototypes built through Foundry (Gather and others)\n\n**Idea as written:** A platform/approach where non-technical people get prototypes built by friends or community builders, via existing tools like Foundry (foundry-app.com) and others. The idea source itself flags this as unconfirmed ('one-line description to confirm'), and customer is listed as 'varies by idea' — both signal the concept is not yet fixed.\n\n### Payer\nNo direct price point exists among the named competitors — Foundry, People.Build, and buildapart.io are all free, pre-revenue beta/landing pages. The closest priced comparable in the adjacent 'founder/builder matching' category is **CoFoundersLab at $29/month** ([source](https://startupgeek.com/best-platforms-to-find-a-co-founder)), which anchors a plausible price hypothesis for a paid tier, though CoFoundersLab has also drawn a class-action complaint over auto-enrolled $89/month charges ([classaction.org](https://www.classaction.org/news/cofounderslab.com-charges-consumers-for-unwanted-monthly-subscription-plans-class-action-says)) — a cautionary data point on billing practices in this category, not a direct claim about O05.\n\n### Competitors\n- **Foundry** (foundry-app.com) — free invite-only beta, 30 active testers, no pricing published. [site](https://www.foundry-app.com/)\n- **People.Build** — free; site currently shows labelled 'Dummy Data' on its 'active teams,' suggesting pre-launch demo content rather than real traction. [site](https://people.build/)\n- **buildapart.io** — adjacent (physical parts/fabrication crowdsourcing, not software prototypes), free to start. [site](https://buildapart.io/)\n- **CoFoundersLab** — $29/mo or $299/yr, the only competitor in this space with confirmed paid conversion. [pricing source](https://startupgeek.com/best-platforms-to-find-a-co-founder)\n- **Pitch An App** — free to pitch/vote; builder takes submitter ideas and pays 5-10% revenue share. [site](https://www.pitchanapp.com/)\n- **Nixbly** — pre-launch landing page, explicitly illustrative (fake) profiles/projects, no pricing. [site](https://nixbly.com/)\n\n### Acquisition channel\nTargeted niche-community outreach (Indie Hackers, builder Slack/Discord) is estimated at **$100-200 per paying customer** versus $500-2,000 in time/money for a Product Hunt launch ([launchedly.app](https://launchedly.app/blog/why-product-hunt-killed-indie-makers), a vendor blog, not independently verified). A full Product Hunt launch alone runs **$200-$2,000** in direct costs plus opportunity cost ([autosaaslaunch.com](https://autosaaslaunch.com/blog/product-hunt-launch-cost-breakdown)).\n\n### Demand evidence\nReal posts show both sides of the pain: people actively looking for someone to build their app ideas (\"Looking for someone to make apps with lmk if your interested or if you know someone who would be interested\" — [reddit via mirror](https://reddit.sentinel-team.org/posts/1vq98vl/snapshots/2026-08-22T03%3A01%3A18.375099Z)), and a direct community rebuttal to the premise itself: \"there is no such thing as an 'ideas guy' anymore. you can just vibe code if you have an idea\" (same thread).\n\n### Case for\nA solo founder with AI agents could replicate the (thin) feature set of existing competitors — profile pages, idea posts, a feed — within six weeks, since those competitors are themselves evidently single-founder landing pages. A genuine 'friend trust network' wedge, if it reduces flakiness versus cold-matching platforms, has at least one paid comparable (CoFoundersLab, $29/mo) proving some willingness to pay in the adjacent co-founder-matching space.\n\n### Case against\nThe niche is saturated with nearly identical, no-traction, single-founder projects launched in the same period (Foundry, People.Build, Nixbly, Pitch An App, CreateThisApp, Somebody Build It, IwantIbuild) — a strong signal either that demand is weak or that the format is a commodity trap. More importantly, the rise of AI 'vibe coding' tools is directly undercutting the core need (non-coders needing other humans to build for them), as voiced by real users in the community. The idea's own description is unresolved ('one-line description to confirm'), which is a weak starting point for committing six weeks.\n\n### Open questions\nSee `open_questions` field — chiefly: what the product actually is, who pays, whether 'friend-sourced' is a real differentiator over existing cold-matching platforms, and whether any named competitor has real (non-dummy) traction."
